## The three items not shown (CONTROL_TRACE.md, re-checked in the stores)

- **Analysts' 82% and 41% NVIDIA revenue growth (q13):** kept in each draw (u1 e86, e131; u2 e97, e141; u3 e88, e135), filed under
  no question. In u1 and u3 one copy was stored by the very call that opened the capex question.
- **Half of 2027 data-centre spend going to memory (q13):** kept in each draw (u1 e145, u2 e155, u3 e149), filed under no question,
  by a later call while the capex question was open.
- **Management's "accelerating growth rates" near $100 billion a quarter (q04):** kept in u1 (e486, unfiled); no record in u2 or u3.
  Its source article left no record in u2; whether it was read is not traced, so no extraction failure is claimed.
- **Both q13 controls agree with reader B without these items** (as does u2/clean-4): delivering them would make the controls count;
  on this record it would not change their answers.

## What would reach 8, within the fixed population (paper bounds)

- An unchanged judge-only re-send: 4.
- Plus u1/clean-6, if both its opened thesis (period 2028) and the ladder step's question and day matched the ruler and its shown
  material held: 5. A ladder repair alone cannot supply it; this is an unproved combined change.
- Plus the three eligible controls, if their items were delivered: 8, which would exercise the line, not pass it (all but one of the
  eight must agree). u3/clean-5 and u1/clean-11 also lack material and would need their own traced changes.
- Each step assumes the readers' marks carry over to changed questions, days and requests; none is demonstrated.

## What this means for the next step

No unchanged judge-only paid send of Stage B is proposed. Any later Stage B test on a changed upstream version or another population
needs its own reviewed design and decision, and a reconciliation with the frame's "no further paid test on the frozen ruler". The
12 of 15 agreement with reader B includes differently worded questions: it is an overlap with the reference, not evidence that those
answers are right, and the four counted controls alone cannot establish the judge's quality.

codex
**Take the Taiwan account as the next bounded diagnostic; do not yet adopt a general company-delay reading or claim that the remaining gate failures are mostly Alex’s rulings.**

1. **The Taiwan case merits the model’s account.** The stored rung says the authority will not adopt tighter controls **by the due day**; both third readers failed it. The account can help locate the instruction the model applied, but it remains the model’s retrospective explanation, not proof of cause. Also, D420 expressly concerns an authority’s proceeding seeking a published conclusion; Taiwan’s prospective rule adoption is not automatically that case. The broader D409/D416 distinction between a decision against an event and continued waiting is the relevant boundary to examine. [Census](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/postponement/RESULT.md>), [D420](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4638>)

2. **Do not record the proposed company-delay rule as settled yet.** The 29/37 agreement is a lead, but the sends include repeats and the readers disagree on some cases. A formal decision to defer beyond a deadline can be read as a decision against the *deadline question*; it can also leave the underlying event pending. D409 distinguishes “ruled out” from waiting, but the record cited does not settle that boundary for every deadline question. Work through representative full questions, the announcement and what the closed card would say, including a positive and a counterexample, before recommending a reversible interpretation. [D409](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4502>), [tally](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/postponement/frozen-step-tally.txt>)

3. **Qualify the gate summary.** The fresh comparison stopped and contained five topics; its L failure was on a development matter. XR and D disputes affect the strict count, but the record does not establish that the other faults are resolved or that Deck 1 will calibrate the families’ interpretation of this event boundary. A one-page status for Alex is useful if it preserves those limits. [Fresh result](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/RESULT-FRESH-A2.md>)

The Stage B shortfall remains worth designing, but D576’s four-to-eight paper bound is **exercise, not a demonstrated pass**. Any alternative control population or change to the frozen ruler needs its own reviewed design; it should not be presumed council-authorized merely because the current line is blocked. [Stage B census](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/stage-b-controls/CENSUS.md>)

CONSENSUS: no, because the Taiwan case does not automatically fall under D420, and the proposed general treatment of announced delays needs representative cases and a resolved boundary before it is recorded as a rule.
